{"level":"error","message":"AI returned invalid JSON in generateProblem","raw":"{\n  \"title\": \"Optimal Playlist Scheduler\",\n  \"slug\": \"optimal-playlist-scheduler\",\n  \"description\": \"You are a music streaming service developer tasked with creating an optimal playlist scheduler. Given a list of songs with their durations and a target playlist duration, your goal is to select a subset of songs such that the total duration is as close as possible to the target duration without exceeding it. If multiple subsets have the same total duration, choose the one with the maximum number of songs. If there are still ties, choose the subset with the smallest lexicographical order of song indices.\n\nInput:\n- A list of integers `durations` (1 <= len(durations) <= 100) representing the durations of the songs in seconds.\n- An integer `target` (1 <= target <= 10000) representing the target playlist duration in seconds.\n\nOutput:\n- A list of integers representing the indices of the selected songs in the optimal playlist. The indices should be 0-based and in ascending order.\n\nConstraints:\n- 1 <= durations[i] <= 1000\n- 1 <= target <= 10000\n\nExamples:\nExample 1:\nInput: durations = [3, 1, 2, 4], target = 5\nOutput: \"0,1,2\"\nExplanation: The subset [3, 1, 2] has a total duration of 6, which is the closest to the target 5 without exceeding it. It also has the maximum number of songs and the smallest lexicographical order of indices.\n\nExample 2:\nInput: durations = [5, 4, 10, 2], target = 11\nOutput: \"0,1,3\"\nExplanation: The subset [5, 4, 2] has a total duration of 11, which exactly matches the target. It also has the maximum number of songs and the smallest lexicographical order of indices.\",\n  \"referenceSolution\": \"def optimal_playlist_scheduler(durations, target):\\n    n = len(durations)\\n    dp = [[(0, 0) for _ in range(target + 1)] for _ in range(n + 1)]\\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\\n        for j in range(1, target + 1):\\n            if durations[i - 1] > j:\\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]\\n            else:\\n                prev_duration, prev_count = dp[i - 1][j]\\n                new_duration, new_count = dp[i - 1][j - durations[i - 1]]\\n                if new_duration + durations[i - 1] > j:\\n                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]\\n                elif new_duration + durations[i - 1] == prev_duration:\\n                    dp[i][j] = (new_duration + durations[i - 1], max(new_count + 1, prev_count))\\n                else:\\n                    dp[i][j] = (new_duration + durations[i - 1], new_count + 1)\\n    result_duration, result_count = dp[n][target]\\n    result_indices = []\\n    for i in range(n, 0, -1):\\n        if dp[i][target][0] != dp[i - 1][target][0]:\\n            result_indices.append(i - 1)\\n            target -= durations[i - 1]\\n    result_indices.sort()\\n    return ','.join(map(str, result_indices))\",\n  \"testCases\": [\n    {\"input\": \"[3, 1, 2, 4], 5\", \"expected\": \"0,1,2\", \"isHidden\": false},\n    {\"input\": \"[5, 4, 10, 2], 11\", \"expected\": \"0,1,3\", \"isHidden\": false},\n    {\"input\": \"[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 10\", \"expected\": \"0,1,2,3\",...","service":"code-execution-backend api","timestamp":"2026-06-01T13:57:41.394Z"}
